WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.002
Sure enough, two men stood outside the stockade.

2
00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:09.349
One waved a white cloth, and the other, waiting calmly, was Long John.

3
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:13.445
It was still quite early in the morning and bone-chillingly cold;

4
00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:17.934
Long John and his companion stood knee-deep in a low, white fog.

5
00:00:17.935 --> 00:00:23.149
The island seemed damp and feverish, a most unhealthy place.

6
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.859
"Keep indoors with your muskets, men," said Captain Smollett.

7
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:33.849
"It may be a trick." Then the captain yelled to the pirates, "Who goes there? Speak or we’ll fire!"

8
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:37.665
"Flag of truce," Silver cried.

9
00:00:37.666 --> 00:00:43.796
The other man shouted, "Captain Silver is here, sir, to make peace."

10
00:00:43.797 --> 00:00:47.512
"Captain Silver? Who’s he?" roared Smollett.

11
00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:52.652
Then he added to himself, "Captain, is it? He’s given himself a promotion!"

12
00:00:52.653 --> 00:00:56.350
"It’s me, sir," Long John called out.

13
00:00:56.351 --> 00:01:03.540
"These lads chose me to be their captain, after you deserted your crew, sir."

14
00:01:03.541 --> 00:01:07.035
He emphasized the word "deserted."

15
00:01:07.036 --> 00:01:12.119
"We want to discuss our differences with you," Silver went on.

16
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:18.201
"All I ask, Captain Smollett, is your word that I’ll be safe inside this stockade,

17
00:01:18.202 --> 00:01:23.843
and that you’ll give me one minute to get out of range before a gun is fired."

18
00:01:23.844 --> 00:01:26.950
"If you wish to talk to me, you can come in," Smollett said.

19
00:01:26.951 --> 00:01:32.620
"I give you my word that if there’s any treachery, it’ll be from you, not me."

20
00:01:32.621 --> 00:01:39.245
"That’s good enough, Captain," Long John said cheerily. "Your word is enough."

21
00:01:39.246 --> 00:01:44.295
Silver walked to the stockade, threw his crutch over, got a leg up from his companion,

22
00:01:44.296 --> 00:01:49.699
and with great vigor and skill, climbed over the fence and dropped safely to the other side.

23
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.002
He had trouble getting up the hill, however.

24
00:01:53.003 --> 00:01:58.396
The tree stumps and the soft sand made him and his crutch as helpless as a ship out of water.

25
00:01:58.397 --> 00:02:06.981
But he stuck to it and arrived, dressed in his best blue coat with a fine laced hat on his head, at last before the captain.

26
00:02:06.982 --> 00:02:13.036
I was so fascinated by the meeting that I left my place at the musket hole and stood behind the captain.

27
00:02:13.037 --> 00:02:17.185
"Have a seat," said Captain Smollett, motioning to the sandy ground.

28
00:02:17.186 --> 00:02:24.082
"Can’t we go inside, Captain?" pleaded Long John. "It’s too cold to sit outside."

29
00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:26.360
"It’s your own doing," the captain said.

30
00:02:26.361 --> 00:02:31.600
"If you had stayed an honest man, you’d be warming yourself in the ship’s galley as my cook.

31
00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:38.571
But you are Captain Silver now, a common mutineer and pirate, so you can go hang!"

32
00:02:38.572 --> 00:02:44.860
"Well, you’ll have to help me up again, that’s all," said Silver as he sat down on the sand.

33
00:02:44.861 --> 00:02:51.319
"If you have anything to say, you’d better say it," the captain replied, sitting down across from him.

34
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:57.814
"Well now, that was quite a fight last night, Captain Smollett," Silver replied.

35
00:02:57.815 --> 00:03:01.706
"I don’t deny it. That’s why I’m here to make peace.

36
00:03:01.707 --> 00:03:07.172
You thrashed my men pretty good—even killed one of us.

37
00:03:07.173 --> 00:03:11.671
But hear me, Captain—you won’t catch us twice, by

38
00:03:11.672 --> 00:03:15.368
thunder! Maybe you think we were all

39
00:03:15.369 --> 00:03:20.040
drunk. But I wasn’t—I was sober, just very

40
00:03:20.041 --> 00:03:25.559
tired. If I’d awakened a second sooner, I’d have pounded

41
00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.635
you." "Well?" Smollett said

42
00:03:28.636 --> 00:03:34.303
coolly. Captain Smollett didn’t know what Silver was talking about, but you’d never have guessed it from his

43
00:03:34.304 --> 00:03:38.617
tone. I had a good inkling, though, as to what Long John was

44
00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:44.252
describing. Ben Gunn must have sneaked up on the pirates while they all lay drunk on the beach around their

45
00:03:44.253 --> 00:03:50.299
fire. I smiled and thought, "Now we have one less pirate to deal with, thanks to my new

46
00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:57.253
friend." "We want that treasure, and we will have it!" exclaimed Silver. "You have the map, don’t

47
00:03:57.254 --> 00:03:59.956
you?" "Maybe," the captain

48
00:03:59.957 --> 00:04:03.737
replied. "Oh, you have it. I know that," said Long

49
00:04:03.738 --> 00:04:08.319
John. "We just want the map. I never meant to do you any

50
00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.496
harm—" "We know what you meant to do," Captain Smollett

51
00:04:13.497 --> 00:04:20.507
interrupted. "Give us the treasure map," demanded Silver, "and stop shooting my men and smashing their heads in while they

52
00:04:20.508 --> 00:04:27.285
sleep. Once we have the treasure, I promise to drop you ashore somewhere safe. Or you can stay

53
00:04:27.286 --> 00:04:37.430
here. We’ll evenly divide the provisions we have, and I promise to send the first ship I see to pick you up. You couldn’t get a better

54
00:04:37.431 --> 00:04:41.681
deal." Captain Smollett rose. "Is that all?" he

55
00:04:41.682 --> 00:04:48.573
said. "Refuse my offer," Silver answered, "and all you’ll see from me is musket

56
00:04:48.574 --> 00:04:52.875
fire." "Very good," the captain said. "Here’s my

57
00:04:52.876 --> 00:05:00.961
counteroffer. You all come here one by one, unarmed. I’ll put you in chains and take you to England for a fair

58
00:05:00.962 --> 00:05:05.033
trial. Refuse my offer, and I’ll see you all at the bottom of the

59
00:05:05.034 --> 00:05:12.695
sea. You can’t find the treasure. You can’t sail the ship—not a man among you knows how to navigate that ship.

60
00:05:12.696 --> 00:05:16.629
You can’t fight us—Gray, there, got away from five of you.

61
00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:22.075
Your ship is useless, Mr. Silver, because it’s anchored on a windless shore.

62
00:05:22.076 --> 00:05:27.428
And in the name of heaven, I’ll put a bullet in your back when I meet you again. Now go.

63
00:05:27.429 --> 00:05:31.409
Get out of here, and make it quick."

64
00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:36.606
Silver’s eyes bulged with wrath. "Help me up!" he shouted.

65
00:05:36.607 --> 00:05:39.630
"No," the captain calmly replied.

66
00:05:39.631 --> 00:05:43.447
"Who’ll give me a hand up?" he roared.

67
00:05:43.448 --> 00:05:52.928
Not one of us moved. Growling foul curses, Long John crawled along the sand to the porch and hoisted himself up on his crutch.

68
00:05:52.929 --> 00:05:56.674
Then he spat into the spring.

69
00:05:56.675 --> 00:05:59.887
"That’s what I think of you," he cried.

70
00:05:59.888 --> 00:06:06.676
"I’ll smash this house like a cask of rum. Laugh, by thunder, laugh!

71
00:06:06.677 --> 00:06:11.390
Those of you that die will be the lucky ones."

72
00:06:11.391 --> 00:06:16.707
He stumbled off, cursing, and then tried four or five times to get over the stockade fence.

73
00:06:16.708 --> 00:06:25.283
The man with the flag of truce finally helped him, and they disappeared among the trees.

